WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:07.040
Centuries ago, astronomers carefully tracked the motion of the moon and planets

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.380
in the ancient city of Babylon,

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.980
Jupiter was a particular favorite because
it represented their main god, Marduk.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:20.220
Babylonian astronomers meticulously recorded their
observations in cuneiform on clay tablets,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.350
using simple mathematics.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:24.880
But the recent translation of several tablets

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.539
shows that the Babylonians did something more.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:34.360
Sometime between 350 and 50 BCE,  Babylonian astronomers used  sophisticated

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:37.040
geometry to track the motion of Jupiter,

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.570
14 centuries earlier than when historians had first thought such techniques

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:43.930
were developed.

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:47.909
To compute Jupiter’s position, they focused
on its movement along the ecliptic.

00:00:47.909 --> 00:00:51.529
The ecliptic is the path that the sun appears
to trace through the stars.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.940
The Babylonians charted Jupiter’s daily
motion against time

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:59.680
The chart produces a trapezoid--with the area
of the trapezoid showing how far along

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.300
the ecliptic Jupiter had traveled in a given amount of time.

00:01:03.309 --> 00:01:06.690
Although the tablets show no actual drawings
of trapezoids,

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:12.170
the calculations demonstrate that the Babylonians
were in effect computing the area under a

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:14.780
curve to achieve an astronomical result.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:18.670
That feat not only demonstrates the acumen
of these early astronomers but

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:24.330
foreshadows the 17th century invention of
calculus, the mathematical backbone of modern physics.

